{
  "dom_id": "step-52179",
  "name": "Example: \n                    Solving Inequalities by Clearing a Rational Expression",
  "problem": "Solve the inequality $\\dfrac{2-5y}{3} \\le 4.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2-5y}{3} & \\le 4 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2-5y}{3}) & \\le 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2-5y}{\\cancel{3}}) & \\le 3\\cdot 4 \\\\ 2-5y & \\le 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles. \n\nRemember, when we divide an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-5y & \\le 12 \\\\ 2-5y-2 & \\le 12-2 \\\\ -5y & \\le 10 \\\\ \\frac{-5y}{-5} & \\,\\,\\ge \\,\\,\\frac{10}{-5} \\\\ y & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $y \\ge -2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
